Let $A \in \Cl^{2}(V, Q)$ with $A^{2} = \lambda \in \R$ (true for every simple bivector $A = a \wedge b$, and for every bivector when $n \le 3$). Then $A^{2k} = \lambda^{k}$, $A^{2k+1} = \lambda^{k} A$, and $e^{A} = \sum_k A^{k}/k!$ is
\begin{equation}
e^{A} =
\begin{cases}
\cos\theta + \dfrac{\sin\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = -\theta^{2} < 0,\\[6pt]
\cosh\theta + \dfrac{\sinh\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = \theta^{2} > 0,\\[6pt]
1 + A, & \lambda = 0 .
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In each case $e^{A} (e^{A})^{\sim} = e^{A} e^{-A} = 1$. For orthogonal $a, b$ with $Q(a) \ne 0$, $e^{\phi\, \hat{a}\hat{b}}$ (unit $\hat{a}, \hat{b}$, or $\hat{b}$ null) is the product of two invertible vectors, hence a rotor (\claimref{obj.std-algebra.rotor}): a rotation when $(a \wedge b)^{2} < 0$, a boost when $> 0$, and a null rotation or translation when $= 0$.

For $A, C \in \Cl^{2}(V, Q)$ and $x \in V$, with the commutator product $A \times X = \tfrac12(AX - XA)$:
\begin{equation}
A \times C = \langle A C \rangle_2 \in \Cl^{2}, \qquad A \times x = \langle A x \rangle_1 \in V, \qquad B(A \times x, y) + B(x, A \times y) = 0 .
\end{equation}
So $\Cl^{2}$ is a Lie algebra under $\times$, and $A \mapsto (x \mapsto A \times x)$ is a Lie algebra homomorphism $\Cl^{2} \to \so(V, Q)$, the skew-adjoint maps of $(V, Q)$. When $(V, Q)$ is nondegenerate it is an isomorphism, so $\dim \so(V, Q) = \binom{n}{2}$. The scalar part is cyclic: $\langle X Y \rangle_0 = \langle Y X \rangle_0$.

The Clifford algebra of any hyperbolic plane $H$ is isomorphic to $\Mat_2(\R)$: if $(u, w)$ is a hyperbolic pair spanning $H$, the assignment
\begin{equation}
u \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad w \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
extends to an isomorphism $\Cl(H) \to \Mat_2(\R)$, under which $uw$ and $wu$ are the complementary idempotents $\operatorname{diag}(1, 0)$ and $\operatorname{diag}(0, 1)$. In particular $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$. Also $\Cl(2,0) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$, by $e_1 \mapsto \operatorname{diag}(1, -1)$, $e_2 \mapsto \begin{psmallmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{psmallmatrix}$.

In $\Cl(p, q, r)$, $n = p + q + r$, with unit pseudoscalar $I$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $I X_k = (-1)^{k(n-1)} X_k I$ for $X_k \in \Cl^{k}$; so $I$ commutes with $\Cl^{+}$, and with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is odd, while it anticommutes with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is even. Equivalently $I X = X I$ for $n$ odd and $I X = \hat{X} I$ for $n$ even.
\item[(ii)] $I \tilde{I} = \prod_{i} Q(e_i)$, which is $(-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ and $0$ when $r > 0$.
\item[(iii)] $I^{2} = (-1)^{n(n-1)/2} (-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ (\claimref{primer.sign}), and $I^{2} = 0$ when $r > 0$: in a degenerate algebra the pseudoscalar is nilpotent.
\end{enumerate}

In the Fock model of \claimref{std-algebra.fock-representation}, for a nonzero spinor $\psi \in \Lambda W$ let $N_\psi = \{x \in V : x \psi = 0\}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $N_\psi$ is totally null, so $\dim N_\psi \le k$; $\psi$ is \emph{pure} when $\dim N_\psi = k$;
\item[(ii)] every maximal totally null subspace of $V$ is $N_\psi$ for a pure $\psi$, unique up to a scalar;
\item[(iii)] (Cartan) for $k \le 3$ every nonzero spinor of definite parity is pure; for $k = 4$, $1 + w_1 w_2 w_3 w_4$ is not;
\item[(iv)] (Thouless form) for every antisymmetric $k \times k$ matrix $Z$, $\psi_Z = \exp\bigl(\sum_{i<j} Z_{ij}\, w_i \wedge w_j\bigr)$ is pure, with $N_{\psi_Z}$ spanned by $v_i - \sum_j Z_{ij} w_j$.
\end{enumerate}

In $\Cl(V, Q)$, for all $u, v \in V$,
\begin{equation}
v^{2} = Q(v), \qquad u v + v u = 2 B(u, v).
\end{equation}
In particular $u v = B(u, v) + \tfrac12(u v - v u)$, the sum of a scalar and an antisymmetric part.

Let $v_1, \dots, v_k, w_1, \dots, w_k \in V$ be a \emph{Witt basis} of their span: $B(v_i, v_j) = B(w_i, w_j) = 0$ and $B(v_i, w_j) = \tfrac12 \delta_{ij}$. Then in $\Cl(V, Q)$
\begin{equation}
v_i v_j + v_j v_i = 0, \qquad w_i w_j + w_j w_i = 0, \qquad v_i w_j + w_j v_i = \delta_{ij},
\end{equation}
the canonical anticommutation relations for $k$ modes (read $v_i$ as annihilators, $w_i$ as creators). The elements $N_i = w_i v_i$ are mutually commuting idempotents with $N_i + v_i w_i = 1$. In particular, for orthogonal hyperbolic planes $\operatorname{span}(e_i, e_{k+i})$ of $\R^{k,k}$, $v_i = \tfrac12(e_i + e_{k+i})$ and $w_i = \tfrac12(e_i - e_{k+i})$ form such a basis. The form need not be nondegenerate outside the span.

With trivial coefficients, $H^1(N_v;\, U) = \mathrm{Hom}(N_v, U)$, one-coboundaries vanishing; and the class of the pseudo-inverse torsor is the tautological homomorphism $\theta$, which is nonzero whenever $U \neq 0$. An $N_v$-invariant pseudo-inverse of $v$ exists, in the given extension, if and only if this class vanishes, if and only if $U = 0$, i.e.\ $n = 2$: the hyperbolic atom of Remark \claimref{note.transport.hyperbolic-atom} is recovered as the exact vanishing locus of the class. Three consequences:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Enlargement-independence, cohomologically.} The group $N_v$, the coefficient module $U$, and the class $\theta$ are all constructed inside $\Cl(\mathbb{V})$ and act identically in every extension; the obstruction is pulled back unchanged along every ascent, which is the cohomological restatement of the quantification over all ascents in Theorem \claimref{transport.no-go-level-elements}.
\item \emph{Levi equivariance.} By Lemma \claimref{transport.group-null-rotations}, $\theta$ intertwines the conjugation action of $\Spin(U)$ on $N_v$ with its vector action on $U$: the class is equivariant for the Levi factor of the stabiliser.
\item \emph{The full stabiliser.} A pseudo-inverse selection equivariant under any subgroup of the stabiliser of $v$ containing $N_v$, in particular under the full parabolic stabiliser of the null direction, would restrict to an $N_v$-fixed point; nonvanishing of $\theta$ on the radical therefore obstructs every enlargement of the symmetry group at once.
\end{enumerate}

In the setting of Theorem \claimref{transport.totality}, represent elements of the quadric by homogeneous triples $(s, x, t)$ with $s,t \in \R$ and $x \in \mathbb{V}$, standing for $N = s\,\nO + x + t\,\nInf$ and defined up to overall scale, subject to the incidence (nullity) condition
\begin{equation}
Q(x) = 2st .
\end{equation}
The ordinary points are the classes with $s \neq 0$, normal form $(1, x, \tfrac12 Q(x))$, i.e.\ $X(x) \;=\; \nO + x + \tfrac12 Q(x)\, \nInf$. The ideal points, the classes with $s = 0$, then satisfy $Q(x) = 0$ and fall into three disjoint strata:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $t \neq 0$ and $x \neq 0$: unique representative $(0, x', \tfrac12)$ with $x' = x/(2t)$ null; these classes are labelled bijectively by the null \emph{vectors} of $\mathbb{V}$, and are exactly the values $\iota$ takes on lifted null points (Theorem \claimref{transport.totality}(3));
\item[(ii)] $t = 0$, $x \neq 0$: the class retains only the null \emph{ray} $[x]$; these classes are labelled by the null directions of $\mathbb{V}$;
\item[(iii)] $x = 0$, which given $s = 0$ forces $t \neq 0$: the single classical point at infinity $[\nInf]$, which is the value $\iota[X(0)]$ by Theorem \claimref{transport.totality}(3).
\end{enumerate}
In these coordinates the map $\iota$ of Theorem \claimref{transport.totality} is the linear involution
\[
\iota(s, x, t) = (2t,\, x,\, \tfrac12 s),
\]
which preserves the incidence condition, is total, and recovers the reciprocal upon normalising the first slot where possible; where normalisation is impossible (first slot zero) the output is an ideal point.

Let $(\mathbb{V}, Q)$ be nondegenerate, with the completion $\Cl(p{+}1,q{+}1)$ and quadric $\mathcal{Q}$ of the section ``The completion: the conformal model''. Define $\iota : \mathcal{Q} \to \mathcal{Q}$ by $\iota[N] = [\,e_+ N e_+\,]$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\iota$ is well defined, total and continuous on $\mathcal{Q}$, and an involution.
\item For every $x$ with $Q(x) \neq 0$, \; $\iota[X(x)] = [X(x/Q(x))]$: on invertible vectors $\iota$ is the geometric reciprocal $\rho$.
\item For every $x_0$ with $Q(x_0) = 0$, that is, a null vector or the origin, $\iota[X(x_0)] = [\, x_0 + \tfrac12 \nInf \,]$, an ideal point (for $x_0 = 0$, the classical point at infinity $[\nInf]$), and this value is the limit of $\iota[X(x)]$ along every path of invertible $x \to x_0$. Consequently $\iota$ is the unique continuous map $\mathcal{Q} \to \mathcal{Q}$ agreeing with the lifted reciprocal $[X(x)] \mapsto [X(\rho(x))]$ on the lifted invertible locus.
\end{enumerate}

