|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} \;=\; \frac{m^{2}}{2} - \frac{\Lambda}{6}\,C \;=\; \frac{\Lambda}{6}\Bigl(\nu^{2} + \frac{9}{4} - C\Bigr),
\end{equation}
the second form for a composite on the principal series, $w = -\tfrac32 \pm i\nu$. Consequently $B_{\Lambda} \ne 0$ if and only if $m^{2} > \Lambda C/3$, which for $C$ of order one is every composite heavier than the Hubble scale; the flat bracket $\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$ is then nonzero up to the $\Lambda$-suppressed $\omega$-area of the join. The identity is a rewriting of the join and says nothing about a vacuum: it converts the matter programme's open equation into the equation the broken phase must satisfy, at consistency level, without supplying the potential that selects the phase, the $W$ and $Z$ masses, or the weak mixing angle. At observation level, and flagged as such: read with Fact \claimref{families.imported-face-valued-bracket-quantum-numbers}, the nonvanishing bracket is a charged singlet with a fixed modulus, the shape of a condensed order parameter, with the reservation recorded in that Fact about what it is an order parameter \emph{of}.

Write the deformed bracket of Fact \claimref{families.imported-dictionary-exact-join} as $B_{\Lambda} = b + \tfrac{\Lambda}{6}\,\Omega_{\omega}$, with $b = \br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$ the flat mass bracket and $\Omega_{\omega} = [\omega^{1}\omega^{2}]$ the composite's $\omega$-bracket, a length since $B_{\Lambda}$ is a mass. Two clauses at two levels.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(Proved.)} The join's leading term expands as
\begin{equation}
2|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} \;=\; 2|b|^{2} \;+\; \frac{2\Lambda}{3}\,\mathrm{Re}\bigl(\bar{b}\,\Omega_{\omega}\bigr) \;+\; \frac{\Lambda^{2}}{18}\,|\Omega_{\omega}|^{2},
\end{equation}
the middle term linear in $b$; its derivative in $b$ at $b = 0$ is $\tfrac{2\Lambda}{3}\Omega_{\omega}$, nonzero iff $\Lambda\Omega_{\omega} \ne 0$; and at fixed moduli the middle term is maximised in the phase of $b$ exactly when $\arg b = \arg\Omega_{\omega}$.
\item \emph{(Reading, at observation level.)} If the effective potential for the flat bracket is built on the mass functional, so that $b$ and the vacuum value of $\Omega_{\omega}$ are treated as independent variables, then the potential contains a $\Lambda$-linear inhomogeneous source for $b$ and $b = 0$ is not an extremum unless the vacuum $\omega$-area vanishes: condensation is not a possibility the potential permits but a tilt it cannot avoid, with the phase of $b$ locked to the phase of $\Omega_{\omega}$ or to its opposite, according to the sign with which the functional enters the potential, which the reading does not fix; either way the locked phase is pure gauge under the abelian factor (Fact \claimref{families.imported-face-valued-bracket-quantum-numbers}), so no physical tension. The value of the condensate is thereby converted into one named object, the vacuum expectation of the $\omega$-area, which the framework defines but does not compute. The reading assumes what a potential for the bracket would have to be; $b$ and $\Omega_{\omega}$ are functions of the same two conformal spinors, and their independence as vacuum variables is the assumption. What the expectation does and does not determine is settled by Proposition \claimref{families.alignment-computed-thirteenth-refutation-democracy-one}.
\end{enumerate}

Let $I_{\Lambda} = n_{\infty} + \lambda n_{0}$ with $\lambda = \Lambda/6$ be the frozen point of Lemma \claimref{mass.de-sitter-scale-frozen-point}, and $Z^{1}, Z^{2} \in \CS$ two conformal spinors (twistors).
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The deformed bracket.)} The pairing of the composite against the frozen point is
\begin{equation}
B_{\Lambda} := \ip{I_{\Lambda}}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}} = \br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} + \frac{\Lambda}{6}\,[\omega^{1}\omega^{2}],
\end{equation}
and at $\Lambda = 0$ it is the classical contraction of the composite with the point at infinity $n_{\infty}$, read as a bivector of $\CS$ (Penrose's infinity twistor).
\item \emph{(The mass.)} The grip $P = M(I_{\Lambda})$ has squared length
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = Q(P) = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + Q(I_{\Lambda})\, C = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + \frac{\Lambda}{3}\, C .
\end{equation}
\item \emph{(The flat shadow.)} At $\Lambda = 0$, $m^{2} = 2|\ip{I}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}}|^{2} = 2|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}|^{2}$: Proposition \claimref{mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-formula}, read as the squared pairing of the composite against the frozen point.
\item \emph{(The point-supported composite.)} If both spinors lie on the line $L_{x}$ of the point $x$, the composite bivector is $\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}X(x)$ and $B_{\Lambda} = \sigma(x)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} = \bigl(1 - \tfrac{\Lambda Q(x)}{12}\bigr)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$: the flat bracket read in the de Sitter scale.
\item \emph{(The massless locus.)} For $\Lambda > 0$, $m^{2} \ge 0$, and $m^{2} = 0$ if and only if $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h$ is a multiple of the identity; that multiple is zero, so the condition is $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h = 0$ (\claimref{synthesis.many-twistor-join}(v)). The doubled composite $Z^{2} = Z^{1}$ has $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $m^{2} = \tfrac{\Lambda}{3}\,(\bar{Z}\cdot Z)^{2}$, so it is massless only when its constituent is null; and there are massless composites that are not doubled, for instance $Z^{1} = e_{1} + e_{4}$, $Z^{2} = \alpha e_{1} + \beta e_{4}$ with $\alpha \neq \beta$.
\end{enumerate}

Every clock of the boundary modes runs at the modular rate $\kappa$, with period $\beta = 2\pi/\kappa = 8\pi M$. Precisely:
\begin{enumerate}
\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{(\roman{enumi})}
\item Call a flow \emph{intrinsic} when it is a one-parameter group of inner automorphisms of the boundary-mode algebra, with even generator, preserving the vector grade. The intrinsic flows of a Clifford boundary-mode algebra are rotor (bivector-generated) flows, quadratic in the pair operators.
\item Call an intrinsic flow a \emph{clock} when it is hyperbolic (its adjoint action on the mode operators semisimple with real eigenvalues) with a common rapidity across the modes it moves, the rapidity being the parameter the geometric normalisation converts to Schwarzschild time. The algebra fixes only the orbits, not the speed (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-modes-modular-inventory}'s reparametrisation clause), so a clock's parameter is normalised to the rapidity itself, not a multiple of it. For a single completed pair the bivector space is one-dimensional, its flow is the boost, and the conversion runs at the surface gravity $\kappa = 1/4M$.
\item A nonzero bivector generates a clock exactly when its adjoint action on the mode operators is semisimple with real eigenvalues, all nonzero ones of equal magnitude, which the normalisation to rapidity sets to one; if $\operatorname{ad}_X$ is semisimple with spectrum $\{+c, 0, -c\}$ then the $\pm c$ eigenspaces are isotropic and mutually dual for the neutral form of the boundary modes, so by Witt extension $X$ is $O(n,n)$-conjugate to $c$ times a sum of standard atom boosts; the geometric normalisation, being a property of the quadratic space rather than of a choice of modes, is invariant under that conjugation and converts every such flow at the one $\kappa$. Writing $E_{ij} = \psi_i^\dagger\psi_j$ for the gauge-invariant bilinears of the $n$-mode boundary space's mode operators $\psi_i$ (Proposition \claimref{scrambling.boundary-mode-space-matrix}, proved independently of this lemma), the cases below are illustrative rather than a partition. The diagonal $E_{ii}$ are the atoms' boosts (Fact \claimref{scrambling.imported-modes-modular-inventory}) and the equal-coefficient sum $\sum_i E_{ii}$ their simultaneous boost, all with adjoint eigenvalues $\pm 1$; the symmetric combinations $E_{ij} + E_{ji}$ are opposed pairs of boosts of the rotated modes $(\psi_i \pm \psi_j)/\sqrt{2}$, again $\pm 1$. The antisymmetric $E_{ij} - E_{ji}$ are compact and the strictly off-diagonal $E_{ij}$ nilpotent, neither carrying a rapidity; a mixed diagonal $\sum_i c_i E_{ii}$ with unequal $|c_i|$ has no common rapidity. And the pairing sector $c\,\psi_i^\dagger\psi_j^\dagger + d\,\psi_j\psi_i$, outside $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$ and completing the bivector algebra of the completed boundary mode space, of dimension $n(2n-1)$ against $\mathfrak{gl}(n)$'s $n^2$ (the $n$ hyperbolic pairs of Fact \claimref{tower.witt-index-quantum-capacity} make the quadratic space split, so that algebra is $\mathfrak{so}(n,n)$), is classified by the sign of one product, its adjoint eigenvalues being $\pm\sqrt{cd}$: hyperbolic for $cd > 0$ (a clock, at unit rapidity outright when $cd = 1$), compact for $cd < 0$, nilpotent for $cd = 0$.
\end{enumerate}
The boundary mode space therefore has many intrinsic flows and one clock rate: whichever clock is read, single-atom, rotated-pair, common, or paired, its period is $\beta$.

Let $A \in \Cl^{2}(V, Q)$ with $A^{2} = \lambda \in \R$ (true for every simple bivector $A = a \wedge b$, and for every bivector when $n \le 3$). Then $A^{2k} = \lambda^{k}$, $A^{2k+1} = \lambda^{k} A$, and $e^{A} = \sum_k A^{k}/k!$ is
\begin{equation}
e^{A} =
\begin{cases}
\cos\theta + \dfrac{\sin\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = -\theta^{2} < 0,\\[6pt]
\cosh\theta + \dfrac{\sinh\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = \theta^{2} > 0,\\[6pt]
1 + A, & \lambda = 0 .
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In each case $e^{A} (e^{A})^{\sim} = e^{A} e^{-A} = 1$. For orthogonal $a, b$ with $Q(a) \ne 0$, $e^{\phi\, \hat{a}\hat{b}}$ (unit $\hat{a}, \hat{b}$, or $\hat{b}$ null) is the product of two invertible vectors, hence a rotor (\claimref{obj.std-algebra.rotor}): a rotation when $(a \wedge b)^{2} < 0$, a boost when $> 0$, and a null rotation or translation when $= 0$.

Let $I$ be the unit pseudoscalar of $\Cl(p, q, r)$. For every signature, right multiplication by $I$ maps $\Cl^{k}$ into $\Cl^{n-k}$, and for $a \in V$
\begin{equation}
a \rfloor (X I) = (a \wedge X) I, \qquad a \wedge (X I) = (a \rfloor X) I .
\end{equation}
If $r = 0$, $I$ is invertible and $X \mapsto X I$ is a linear isomorphism $\Cl^{k} \to \Cl^{n-k}$ (the \emph{duality}); for a nonzero $k$-blade $A$, $A I$ is a nonzero $(n-k)$-blade with $[A I] = [A]^{\perp}$. So outer and inner products are exchanged by duality.

In $\Cl(V, Q)$, a vector $v$ is invertible if and only if $Q(v) \ne 0$, and then $v^{-1} = v / Q(v)$. A nonzero null vector satisfies $v^{2} = 0$: it is a zero divisor. A \emph{versor} is a product $g = u_1 u_2 \cdots u_k$ of invertible vectors; it satisfies
\begin{equation}
g \tilde{g} = \tilde{g} g = Q(u_1) Q(u_2) \cdots Q(u_k) \ne 0,
\end{equation}
so $g$ is invertible with $g^{-1} = \tilde{g} / \bigl(Q(u_1) \cdots Q(u_k)\bigr)$. None of this needs the form to be nondegenerate.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\R \otimes \HH = \HH$, $\C \otimes \C \cong \C \oplus \C$, $\C \otimes \HH \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ and $\HH \otimes \HH \cong \Mat_4(\R)$ (all tensor products over $\R$).
\item[(ii)] For $k = 0, \dots, 8$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|ccccccccc}
$k$ & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\ \hline
$\Cl(k,0)$ & $\R$ & $\R^{2}$ & $\Mat_2(\R)$ & $\Mat_2(\C)$ & $\Mat_2(\HH)$ & $\Mat_2(\HH)^{2}$ & $\Mat_4(\HH)$ & $\Mat_8(\C)$ & $\Mat_{16}(\R)$ \\
$\Cl(0,k)$ & $\R$ & $\C$ & $\HH$ & $\HH^{2}$ & $\Mat_2(\HH)$ & $\Mat_4(\C)$ & $\Mat_8(\R)$ & $\Mat_8(\R)^{2}$ & $\Mat_{16}(\R)$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
where $A^{2} = A \oplus A$.
\item[(iii)] $\Cl(p{+}1, q{+}1) \cong \Mat_2(\Cl(p,q))$ and $\Cl(p{+}8, q) \cong \Cl(p, q{+}8) \cong \Mat_{16}(\Cl(p,q))$. Hence $\Cl(p,q)$ is determined by $n = p + q$ and $p - q \bmod 8$, and is the algebra given in \claimref{primer.classification-real-clifford-algebras}, which this result proves.
\end{enumerate}

In $\Cl(p, q, r)$, $n = p + q + r$, with unit pseudoscalar $I$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $I X_k = (-1)^{k(n-1)} X_k I$ for $X_k \in \Cl^{k}$; so $I$ commutes with $\Cl^{+}$, and with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is odd, while it anticommutes with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is even. Equivalently $I X = X I$ for $n$ odd and $I X = \hat{X} I$ for $n$ even.
\item[(ii)] $I \tilde{I} = \prod_{i} Q(e_i)$, which is $(-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ and $0$ when $r > 0$.
\item[(iii)] $I^{2} = (-1)^{n(n-1)/2} (-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ (\claimref{primer.sign}), and $I^{2} = 0$ when $r > 0$: in a degenerate algebra the pseudoscalar is nilpotent.
\end{enumerate}

In the Fock model of \claimref{std-algebra.fock-representation}, for a nonzero spinor $\psi \in \Lambda W$ let $N_\psi = \{x \in V : x \psi = 0\}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $N_\psi$ is totally null, so $\dim N_\psi \le k$; $\psi$ is \emph{pure} when $\dim N_\psi = k$;
\item[(ii)] every maximal totally null subspace of $V$ is $N_\psi$ for a pure $\psi$, unique up to a scalar;
\item[(iii)] (Cartan) for $k \le 3$ every nonzero spinor of definite parity is pure; for $k = 4$, $1 + w_1 w_2 w_3 w_4$ is not;
\item[(iv)] (Thouless form) for every antisymmetric $k \times k$ matrix $Z$, $\psi_Z = \exp\bigl(\sum_{i<j} Z_{ij}\, w_i \wedge w_j\bigr)$ is pure, with $N_{\psi_Z}$ spanned by $v_i - \sum_j Z_{ij} w_j$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $(V, Q)$ be any real quadratic space (degenerate allowed) and $(W, Q_W)$ a nondegenerate one of even dimension, with pseudoscalar $\omega_W$ and $\omega_W^{2} = \epsilon \in \{1, -1\}$. Then
\begin{equation}
\Cl(V \perp W,\ \epsilon Q \oplus Q_W) \;\cong\; \Cl(V, Q) \otimes_{\R} \Cl(W, Q_W), \qquad v \mapsto v \otimes \omega_W, \quad w \mapsto 1 \otimes w .
\end{equation}
For the planes and for $\R^{0,8}$, $\R^{8,0}$ this gives, with $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Cl(2,0) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$:
\begin{equation}
\Cl(p{+}1, q{+}1) \cong \Mat_2(\Cl(p,q)), \quad \Cl(q{+}2, p) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(2,0), \quad \Cl(q, p{+}2) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(0,2),
\end{equation}
and $\Cl(p, q{+}8) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(0,8)$, $\Cl(p{+}8, q) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(8,0)$; all of these hold with a radical $\R^{0,0,r}$ added to $(p, q)$.

In $\Cl(V, Q)$, for all $u, v \in V$,
\begin{equation}
v^{2} = Q(v), \qquad u v + v u = 2 B(u, v).
\end{equation}
In particular $u v = B(u, v) + \tfrac12(u v - v u)$, the sum of a scalar and an antisymmetric part.

Let $v_1, \dots, v_k, w_1, \dots, w_k \in V$ be a \emph{Witt basis} of their span: $B(v_i, v_j) = B(w_i, w_j) = 0$ and $B(v_i, w_j) = \tfrac12 \delta_{ij}$. Then in $\Cl(V, Q)$
\begin{equation}
v_i v_j + v_j v_i = 0, \qquad w_i w_j + w_j w_i = 0, \qquad v_i w_j + w_j v_i = \delta_{ij},
\end{equation}
the canonical anticommutation relations for $k$ modes (read $v_i$ as annihilators, $w_i$ as creators). The elements $N_i = w_i v_i$ are mutually commuting idempotents with $N_i + v_i w_i = 1$. In particular, for orthogonal hyperbolic planes $\operatorname{span}(e_i, e_{k+i})$ of $\R^{k,k}$, $v_i = \tfrac12(e_i + e_{k+i})$ and $w_i = \tfrac12(e_i - e_{k+i})$ form such a basis. The form need not be nondegenerate outside the span.

Let $(V, Q)$ be a real quadratic space of signature $(p, q, r)$ and let $m = \min(p, q)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] If $P \subseteq V$ is a nondegenerate plane and $u \in P$ is a nonzero null vector, there is a unique $w \in P$ such that $(u, w)$ is a hyperbolic pair; $P$ is a hyperbolic plane.
\item[(ii)] (Witt decomposition) There is an orthogonal decomposition
\begin{equation}
V = H_1 \perp \dots \perp H_m \perp A \perp \operatorname{rad} V
\end{equation}
with each $H_i$ a hyperbolic plane and $A$ anisotropic ($Q(a) \ne 0$ for $a \ne 0$, hence definite, of signature $(p - m, q - m, 0)$).
\item[(iii)] If $(u_i, w_i)$ is a hyperbolic pair spanning $H_i$, then $N = \operatorname{span}(u_1, \dots, u_m) \oplus \operatorname{rad} V$ is a maximal totally null subspace, and every totally null subspace has dimension at most $m + r$.
\end{enumerate}
